Building Effective Agents with JLL GPT
Introduction and AI Agents Definition
Since the inception of JLL GPT, going back to August of 2023, we've worked with dozens of teams building LLM agents both at JLL and with some of our clients and partners.

Consistently, the most successful implementations weren't using complex frameworks or specialized libraries. Instead, they were built with simple, composable patterns.

In this post, we share some lessons from the industry and real-life use cases where teams have been building agents and give practical advice for developers on building effective agents.

What are agents? Definitions, marketing perspectives
No one seems to agree on what an agent is. In some part, that's due to aggressive marketing efforts by the AI vendors (for example, Microsoft rebranding M365 Copilot as "Microsoft AI agents", without adding any new features, or Salesforce rebranding "Einstein Copilot" to "Agentforce"). It seems that by now, most "copilots" in the industry have been rebranded to "agents", often without adding any new "agentic" functionality.

Leading AI vendors' definitions
To agree on a definition for agents and separate reality from the hype, it's useful to look at how leading AI vendors such as OpenAI and Anthropic define agents in their SDKs.

From the OpenAI Agents SDK: "An agent is a large language model (LLM), configured with instructions and tools."

Other, somewhat vague definitions, include things like "AI applications that can autonomously perform tasks". While these definitions can make sense, it's quite difficult to define what "autonomously" actually means. For example, is a research agent like DeepResearch from OpenAI, Google, Perplexity and more, that gathers data to produce a report, autonomous?

As state-of-the-art AI currently revolves around LLMs, in practice, an agentic AI application typically equates to an application making use of tools ("function calling" in AI engineering terms) based on an LLM parsing some input, based on which it routes the control flow, based on its instructions.

Another useful definition is provided by Anthropic, the company behind the Claude series of models. Anthropic makes an interesting distinction between whether the control flow of the application is orchestrated by predefined rules (workflows) or via an LLM call (agents):

AI Workflows are systems where LLMs and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths.
AI Agents, on the other hand, are systems where LLMs dynamically direct their own processes and tool usage, maintaining control over how they accomplish tasks.
Agent Archetypes
Computer-use, Browser-use agents (high generalization)
These agents are capable of many tasks a human could do on a computer, performing basic actions like clicks and keyboard inputs.

This is demonstrated in manus.im for example, a startup company that has built a wrapper on top of Claude 3.5 Sonnet and browser-use open-source project to create nice workflows to automate a variety of tasks: Manus AI Demo

OpenAI's agent SDK, which contains a few built-in tools for the LLM to call, also includes computer-use tool:

https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/tools-computer-use

And of course, Anthropic has one too: Anthropic Computer Use Example

Most applications leveraging computer use – Manus AI, Devin, Operator, Convergence AI and more – have been trending for a short while, but over time, the AI community has realized they are not mature enough for most production use cases, due to some of the problems we will discuss on the LLM limitations section below.

Therefore, we do not recommend this approach at the moment.

Tool use agents (medium generalization)
These agents use mid-level actions that balance flexibility and efficiency, handling tasks like workflow automation and data processing.

A company that has leaned deeply into Tool use agents is Glean, with Glean Agents:

You can also glimpse Glean Agents for Sales use cases here:

Another great agentic product in this archetype is Fyxer AI, which prepares automatic drafts for emails for you, and is claiming to save teams at least 1 hour a day of work by leveraging AI to handle email and calendar related tasks: Fyxer AI Demo

This approach is more reliable than computer-use, but still quite generalizable to many use cases, as a tool/function call could basically mean anything.

Specialized agents (low generalization)
These orchestrator agents coordinate specialized sub-agents, each optimized for specific tasks like customer support, contract drafting, and engineering debugging.

Examples of this are the JLL GPT Tech Support Agent, which is connected to many data sources from which it can retrieve relevant information, and is designed to submit requests on your behalf for tech support issues: Tech Support Agent Demo

When (and when not) to use agents
Automation and AI agents, accuracy and monitoring
People and organizations have been working on automating tasks using customized software, in house applications, no-code platforms, for decades now.

The ability to better understand the input and to more accurately trigger tools based on that understanding greatly enhances our ability to automate processes. This is where AI agents are really changing the game: it's much easier to stitch automations based on inputs.

When building applications with LLMs, we recommend finding the simplest solution possible, and only increasing complexity when needed.

For many applications, optimizing single LLM calls with retrieval and in-context examples is usually enough. In other applications, tool calling may be warranted. And in more rare examples, both routing and tool calling will be required.

Agentic systems often trade latency and cost for better task performance, and you should consider when this tradeoff makes sense.

For example: before LLMs/AI agents, automating a response to a client asking for some information about a real estate property an agent is marketing would typically be rule-based, and as such, less accurate. With LLMs, a broker can use no-code platforms to stitch an end-to-end workflow to respond to clients with accurate information, relying on the LLMs capability to parse the request and call the right tool (e.g. an email sending API).

When this becomes more complicated and potentially impossible to implement, is when accuracy requirements are high. A broker responding to a client with wrong or LLM-hallucinated information can potentially end up in a lawsuit against the company. Therefore, rigorous evaluation and monitoring should be in place.

Depending on the quality bar required from the AI agent, it may be very simple or impossible to implement.

Limitations of AI Agents
Agents are confined by the limitations of LLMs. As probabilistic pattern matching models, LLMs are very far from offering human-level AI capabilities.

In February 2025, Yann LeCun, Turing-award winner and godfather of AI (the underlying technology of LLMs), stated that not only LLMs are not currently as intelligent as humans, but they will likely not be the path for human-level AI, advising researchers who are interested in developing human level AI models to not work on LLMs.

Basic accuracy and data retrieval issues
We have used state of the art models with internet access (like Gemini models with built-in Google Search) to ask the following simple question:

"When do the following companies publish their earnings for the entire year of 2024:
JLL
CBRE
Cushman & Wakefield
Colliers"
This task basically involves Googling each company's earnings report and picking the first or second result from Google to see the date (the date is sometimes given right there on Google).

The following models, all state of the art, could not answer the question correctly:

Gemini Flash 2.5
Gemini Flash 2.5 with Thinking
Some models could provide the right answers, but in real life use cases, one would not know which model to choose in advance.

The wrong answer below provided by some of the models above not only gets the Colliers date wrong, but it also says "confirmed" next to the wrong date, and the Cushman date is missing altogether...

Hallucinations and other limitations
Lack of understanding of basic mathematical concepts and visuals

Since LLMs are based on probabilistic pattern matching from training on language data, they lack the most basic understanding of mathematical concepts, like counting.

The example below is using the state-of-the-art model o1 pro from OpenAI, which is a leading model in terms of benchmarks and is one of OpenAI's most expensive models to date (not available on the free and plus version). It is not capable of solving counting problems at the level of a 4-year-old:

The below example uses o3-mini-high, the high-intelligence version of a state-of-the-art "reasoning" model from OpenAI:

Hallucinations: an LLM feature, not a bug

A well-known issue with LLMs is their tendency to generate hallucinated content: a sometimes hard-to-pick-up answer to some question which is based on completely made-up data, not grounded in reality.

This is in fact a feature of LLMs, not a bug. Since LLMs are models trained to predict the most likely reasonable token of text based on some input, it is the case that when they generate hallucinated content that might misinform the user, they do so because it's indeed the most likely text to generate based on their training data. This is why it's so difficult (and potentially impossible) to solve this issue.

