There is a great write-up by Anthropic, developer of Claude, about the topic of building effective agents. This includes design patterns and more: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents

Conclusion
Building effective agents usually works best when keeping things simple and modular rather than using overly complex systems. AI agents can mean different things to different people, but generally, they are applications powered by LLMs, where the LLM decides what actions to take based on context, and which third party tools (complex or simple) to call. For this reason, depending on the quality bar required from the AI agent, it may be very simple or impossible to implement.

AI agents make automating tasks easier but need careful oversight due to accuracy challenges, since agents still struggle with issues like hallucinations and basic misunderstandings, which come from the fundamental design of current language models.

Using straightforward approaches, like writing your own code without any framework, or using a lightweight one like the OpenAI SDK, usually gives cleaner and more manageable solutions than more complicated frameworks.

